---
Part VI, Chapter 6: The Loom language
Package: packages/loom-lang-services/src/LoomLanguage.ts
Language: TypeScript
---

# The Loom language

`LoomLanguage` gives the **Frame** its editor support. The frame is Loom's own syntax, the layer that composes a loom: its headings, its `::[…]` anchors, its warps, and its specifiers and sinks. `LoomLanguage` is always active, because every `.loom` has a frame.

The service answers from the composition Loom builds, through the `FrameQuery` capability the host provides, and runs no compiler of any language. The code a loom composes belongs to the code's own language service, which answers for it in that language: the TypeScript service gives a TypeScript section its checks, its hover and the outline of its symbols. So the frame and the code never share a compiler, and a loom opens in the editor whether or not any TypeScript is installed.

The root's `languageId` is `loom`, distinct from a product section's `typescript`, so the frame and each section route to their own services. `LoomLanguage` declares no `extensions` — the Frame is the substrate, never a tangle target.

## Loom's own diagnostics

A malformed tag, or an anchor that names no section, is Loom's own health, which no language's compiler can report. `loomDiagnostics` surfaces it.

The diagnostics arrive in `.loom` coordinates, so they belong to the source itself. Volar dispatches a diagnostic request to each of a file's projected documents, and `loomDiagnostics` answers only for the root — the whole-file mirror whose map to the source is the identity. It returns nothing for the prose and the product documents, whose generated spans are not where a source diagnostic lives. For the root it turns the source's uri, which the embedded uri carries, into a location through `FrameQuery`'s `locationOf`, asks `FrameQuery` for that loom's health, adds the names its anchors expect and their sections do not provide, as the next section describes, and turns each Loom diagnostic into Volar's shape. A Loom severity becomes its editor code — `error` is 1, `warning` is 2, `info` is 3 — and a diagnostic's source offsets become a range through the document's own `positionAt`, which lands the squiggle on the exact span the parser flagged.

=>

const lspSeverity = (severity: Diagnostic['severity']): LspDiagnostic['severity'] =>
  severity === 'error' ? 1 : severity === 'warning' ? 2 : 3

const toLspDiagnostic = (
  at: (offset: number) => LspDiagnostic['range']['start'],
  diagnostic: Diagnostic,
): LspDiagnostic => ({
  range: {
    start: at(diagnostic.position.start.offset),
    end: at(diagnostic.position.end.offset),
  },
  message: diagnostic.message,
  severity: lspSeverity(diagnostic.severity),
  source: 'loom',
})

const loomDiagnostics = (frame: FrameQueryApi): LanguageServicePlugin => ({
  name: 'loom-diagnostics',
  capabilities: {
    diagnosticProvider: {
      interFileDependencies: true,
      workspaceDiagnostics: false,
    },
  },
  create: (context) => ({
    provideDiagnostics: async (document) => {
      const decoded = context.decodeEmbeddedDocumentUri(URI.parse(document.uri))
      if (!decoded || decoded[1] !== 'root') return undefined
      const loom = frame.locationOf(decoded[0])
      const at = (offset: number) => document.positionAt(offset)
      const missing = await missingSymbols(
        (uri) => context.getLanguageService().getDocumentSymbols(uri),
        frame,
        loom,
      )
      return pipe(
        [...frame.diagnostics(loom), ...missing],
        Array.map((diagnostic) => toLspDiagnostic(at, diagnostic)),
      )
    },
  }),
})

## Names a section must provide

An anchor may say which names its section provides, as `::[Greeting function]{provides=greet}` does. No Loom pass can tell whether the section's code declares `greet`, because only the section's language knows what its code declares. A language service reports every name a document declares, each one a symbol with a kind and a range, through the protocol's document symbols. So `missingSymbols` asks for them, through Volar, which gathers every symbol a loom's projected documents report and maps each one back into the loom.

`FrameQuery`'s `contracts` gives, for each anchor that states names, the range of the section's code and the names the anchor expects there. `missingSymbols` asks Volar once for the symbols of each loom that holds such a section, and `outermostWithin` keeps the names declared inside the section's code. It takes only the outermost of them, since a method declared inside a class is no name of the section's own. An expected name that none of them carries yields the `MissingSymbol` diagnostic the contract holds. `inside` tells whether one range lies within another, and `before` orders two positions in the editor's coordinates.

The check runs in the editor, where the language services run. A tangle runs no language service, so it never raises `MissingSymbol`, as it never raises a type error either.

=>

type EditorRange = FrameLocation['range']

type EditorPosition = EditorRange['start']

const before = (one: EditorPosition, other: EditorPosition): boolean =>
  one.line < other.line || (one.line === other.line && one.character < other.character)

const inside = (inner: EditorRange, outer: EditorRange): boolean =>
  !before(inner.start, outer.start) && !before(outer.end, inner.end)

const outermostWithin =
  (range: EditorRange) =>
  (symbol: DocumentSymbol): ReadonlyArray<string> =>
    inside(symbol.selectionRange, range)
      ? [symbol.name]
      : Array.flatMap(symbol.children ?? [], outermostWithin(range))

const missingSymbols = async (
  symbolsAt: (uri: URI) => Promise<ReadonlyArray<DocumentSymbol> | undefined>,
  frame: FrameQueryApi,
  loom: string,
): Promise<ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic>> => {
  const contracts = frame.contracts(loom)
  const symbols = new Map(
    await Promise.all(
      Array.map(
        Array.dedupe(Array.map(contracts, (contract) => contract.target.path)),
        async (path) => [path, (await symbolsAt(frame.uriOf(path))) ?? []] as const,
      ),
    ),
  )
  return Array.flatMap(contracts, (contract) => {
    const declared = new Set(
      Array.flatMap(
        Option.getOrElse(Option.fromNullishOr(symbols.get(contract.target.path)), () => []),
        outermostWithin(contract.target.range),
      ),
    )
    return pipe(
      contract.expected,
      Array.filter((expected) => !declared.has(expected.name)),
      Array.map((expected) => expected.diagnostic),
    )
  })
}

## Fixing what a diagnostic reports

A Loom diagnostic may carry fixes, and `loomFixes` offers them as the editor's quick fixes. The editor asks for code actions over a range of a file, and Volar passes the request on to the root, the same whole-file mirror the diagnostics answer for. `loomFixes` reads that loom's diagnostics from `FrameQuery` again and keeps each one whose span `meets` the range.

`fixActions` turns each fix of a kept diagnostic into a `CodeAction` of the `quickfix` kind. The action names the diagnostic it repairs, and its edit writes the fix's text over the diagnostic's span in the loom, or over the span the fix's `at` names. So an unresolved anchor's fix declares its section further down the loom. So the editor lists one action for each path an ambiguous anchor offers, and choosing one writes that path where the anchor's name stood. A fix that names a `file` writes a new loom instead, and `newLoom` gives its edit: it creates the file, refusing one that already exists, and writes the fix's text into it. So a box that points to a chapter nobody has written offers an action that writes the chapter. `writtenFile` gives the edit that writes a file whole, and says whether the file may replace one that exists.

=>

const meets = (diagnostic: Diagnostic, start: number, end: number): boolean =>
  diagnostic.position.start.offset <= end && start <= diagnostic.position.end.offset

const writtenFile = (uri: string, text: string, replaces: boolean): WorkspaceEdit => ({
  documentChanges: [
    { kind: 'create', uri, options: { overwrite: replaces, ignoreIfExists: false } },
    {
      textDocument: { uri, version: null },
      edits: [
        {
          range: { start: { line: 0, character: 0 }, end: { line: 0, character: 0 } },
          newText: text,
        },
      ],
    },
  ],
})

const newLoom = (uri: string, text: string): WorkspaceEdit => writtenFile(uri, text, false)

const fixActions =
  (
    uri: string,
    uriOf: (file: string) => string,
    at: (offset: number) => LspDiagnostic['range']['start'],
  ) =>
  (diagnostic: Diagnostic): ReadonlyArray<CodeAction> => {
    const repaired = toLspDiagnostic(at, diagnostic)
    return Array.map(diagnostic.fixes ?? [], (fix): CodeAction => ({
      title: fix.title,
      kind: 'quickfix',
      diagnostics: [repaired],
      edit: Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(fix.file), {
        onSome: (file) => newLoom(uriOf(file), fix.text),
        onNone: () => ({
          changes: {
            [uri]: [
              {
                range: Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(fix.at), {
                  onNone: () => repaired.range,
                  onSome: (span) => ({ start: at(span.start.offset), end: at(span.end.offset) }),
                }),
                newText: fix.text,
              },
            ],
          },
        }),
      }),
    }))
  }

const loomFixes = (frame: FrameQueryApi): LanguageServicePlugin => ({
  name: 'loom-fixes',
  capabilities: { codeActionProvider: { codeActionKinds: ['quickfix'] } },
  create: (context) => ({
    provideCodeActions: (document, range) => {
      const decoded = context.decodeEmbeddedDocumentUri(URI.parse(document.uri))
      if (!decoded || decoded[1] !== 'root') return undefined
      const loom = frame.locationOf(decoded[0])
      const start = document.offsetAt(range.start)
      const end = document.offsetAt(range.end)
      return pipe(
        frame.diagnostics(loom),
        Array.filter((diagnostic) => meets(diagnostic, start, end)),
        Array.flatMap(
          fixActions(
            frame.uriOf(loom).toString(),
            (file) => frame.uriOf(file).toString(),
            (offset) => document.positionAt(offset),
          ),
        ),
      )
    },
  }),
})

## Navigating the frame

An anchor names a section. Following it to that section, or finding every anchor that composes a section, follows Loom's own edges, which no language's compiler knows. Two plugins answer them, each over the root mirror alone, the whole-file document whose map to the source is the identity, so a position there is a source offset.

`loomDefinition` reads the symbol under the cursor and jumps to the definition its name resolves to. It asks `FrameQuery` for two spans: the target through `definition`, and the whole symbol under the cursor through `navigationRange`. `toLocationLink` pairs them — the definition as the link's target, named by the uri `FrameQuery`'s `uriOf` gives its location, and the symbol as its `originSelectionRange` — so the editor underlines the whole name, an `::[…]` anchor or a `Part I` alike, not the single word beneath the cursor.

=>

const toLocationLink = (
  frame: FrameQueryApi,
  target: FrameLocation,
  origin: FrameLocation | undefined,
): LocationLink => ({
  targetUri: frame.uriOf(target.path).toString(),
  targetRange: target.range,
  targetSelectionRange: target.range,
  originSelectionRange: origin?.range,
})

const loomDefinition = (frame: FrameQueryApi): LanguageServicePlugin => ({
  name: 'loom-definition',
  capabilities: { definitionProvider: true },
  create: (context) => ({
    provideDefinition: (document, position) => {
      const decoded = context.decodeEmbeddedDocumentUri(URI.parse(document.uri))
      if (!decoded || decoded[1] !== 'root') return undefined
      const loom = frame.locationOf(decoded[0])
      const offset = document.offsetAt(position)
      const target = frame.definition(loom, offset)
      return target === undefined
        ? undefined
        : [toLocationLink(frame, target, frame.navigationRange(loom, offset))]
    },
  }),
})

~

`loomReferences` reads a heading or an anchor under the cursor and lists every place its section is named — the heading itself and each anchor that composes it. It returns a `Location` per result, the file keyed by its uri.

=>

const loomReferences = (frame: FrameQueryApi): LanguageServicePlugin => ({
  name: 'loom-references',
  capabilities: { referencesProvider: true },
  create: (context) => ({
    provideReferences: (document, position) => {
      const decoded = context.decodeEmbeddedDocumentUri(URI.parse(document.uri))
      if (!decoded || decoded[1] !== 'root') return undefined
      return pipe(
        frame.references(frame.locationOf(decoded[0]), document.offsetAt(position)),
        Array.map((location) => ({
          uri: frame.uriOf(location.path).toString(),
          range: location.range,
        })),
      )
    },
  }),
})

## Renaming a section

A Loom section's name lives in its heading and in every anchor that composes it, so a rename must rewrite them all as one symbol. `loomRename` renames the section that way. `provideRenameRange` gives the editor the span its rename box covers — the title or anchor name under the cursor — so the box wraps the whole name. `provideRenameEdits` returns the edit: it asks `FrameQuery` for every span the rename touches, and `groupEdits` turns them into a workspace edit, keyed by each file's uri. Each span takes the new name, unless it carries a text of its own, as the old name a concept keeps among its forms does.

=>

const groupEdits = (
  frame: FrameQueryApi,
  locations: ReadonlyArray<FrameEdit>,
  newName: string,
): Record<string, TextEdit[]> =>
  Object.fromEntries(
    Object.entries(
      Array.groupBy(locations, (location) => frame.uriOf(location.path).toString()),
    ).map(([uri, locs]) => [
      uri,
      locs.map((location) => ({ range: location.range, newText: location.text ?? newName })),
    ]),
  )

const loomRename = (frame: FrameQueryApi): LanguageServicePlugin => ({
  name: 'loom-rename',
  capabilities: { renameProvider: { prepareProvider: true } },
  create: (context) => ({
    provideRenameRange: (document, position) => {
      const decoded = context.decodeEmbeddedDocumentUri(URI.parse(document.uri))
      if (!decoded || decoded[1] !== 'root') return undefined
      const span = frame.renameRange(frame.locationOf(decoded[0]), document.offsetAt(position))
      return span === undefined ? undefined : span.range
    },
    provideRenameEdits: (document, position, newName) => {
      const decoded = context.decodeEmbeddedDocumentUri(URI.parse(document.uri))
      if (!decoded || decoded[1] !== 'root') return undefined
      const edits = frame.rename(frame.locationOf(decoded[0]), document.offsetAt(position))
      return edits.length === 0 ? undefined : { changes: groupEdits(frame, edits, newName) }
    },
  }),
})

## A concept's card

A link in prose may name a concept, and a reader resting on the link wants the concept's meaning without leaving the sentence. `loomHover` shows it. It answers for the root alone, like the navigation, and asks `FrameQuery` for the card of the concept under the cursor. The card becomes Volar's `Hover`, its Markdown drawn over the whole link or heading the cursor rests on.

=>

const loomHover = (frame: FrameQueryApi): LanguageServicePlugin => ({
  name: 'loom-hover',
  capabilities: { hoverProvider: true },
  create: (context) => ({
    provideHover: (document, position): Hover | undefined => {
      const decoded = context.decodeEmbeddedDocumentUri(URI.parse(document.uri))
      if (!decoded || decoded[1] !== 'root') return undefined
      const card = frame.hover(frame.locationOf(decoded[0]), document.offsetAt(position))
      return card === undefined
        ? undefined
        : { contents: { kind: 'markdown', value: card.markdown }, range: card.range }
    },
  }),
})

## A word two concepts claim

A plain word that one concept claims reads as a link to the concept, and the card and the definition above answer for it too. A word that two concepts claim is recognised as neither, as the chapter "The scope of a concept" describes. `loomLinks` offers the author a link to each concept instead, among the editor's quick fixes, and the author picks the one the sentence means. It answers for the root alone, like `loomFixes`, and asks `FrameQuery` for the links offered over the range the editor names. Each becomes a `CodeAction` of the `quickfix` kind, and `workspaceEditOf` gathers its edits by file into the edit the action applies.

=>

const workspaceEditOf = (frame: FrameQueryApi, fix: FrameFix): WorkspaceEdit => ({
  changes: groupEdits(frame, fix.edits, ''),
})

const loomLinks = (frame: FrameQueryApi): LanguageServicePlugin => ({
  name: 'loom-links',
  capabilities: { codeActionProvider: { codeActionKinds: ['quickfix'] } },
  create: (context) => ({
    provideCodeActions: (document, range) => {
      const decoded = context.decodeEmbeddedDocumentUri(URI.parse(document.uri))
      if (!decoded || decoded[1] !== 'root') return undefined
      return Array.map(
        frame.links(
          frame.locationOf(decoded[0]),
          document.offsetAt(range.start),
          document.offsetAt(range.end),
        ),
        (fix): CodeAction => ({
          title: fix.title,
          kind: 'quickfix',
          edit: workspaceEditOf(frame, fix),
        }),
      )
    },
  }),
})

## Approving a reference

A reference into another loom may be approved, as the chapter "Fingerprints on relations" describes. A drifted reference is approved again once the author has read what changed, and a reference nobody approved is tracked from then on. `loomApprovals` offers both among the editor's quick fixes, for the references within the range the editor names. It answers for the root alone, like `loomFixes`.

An approval changes no loom. It changes what the workspace keeps of its relations, and a workspace on a disk keeps them in a file. So the action's edit writes that file whole, with the text `FrameQuery` gives, through `writtenFile`. The author sees the edit and can undo it, like any other. A workspace that keeps its relations in no file offers no approval.

=>

const loomApprovals = (frame: FrameQueryApi): LanguageServicePlugin => ({
  name: 'loom-approvals',
  capabilities: { codeActionProvider: { codeActionKinds: ['quickfix'] } },
  create: (context) => ({
    provideCodeActions: (document, range) => {
      const decoded = context.decodeEmbeddedDocumentUri(URI.parse(document.uri))
      if (!decoded || decoded[1] !== 'root') return undefined
      return Array.map(
        frame.approvals(
          frame.locationOf(decoded[0]),
          document.offsetAt(range.start),
          document.offsetAt(range.end),
        ),
        (approval): CodeAction => ({
          title: approval.title,
          kind: 'quickfix',
          edit: writtenFile(frame.uriOf(approval.file).toString(), approval.text, true),
        }),
      )
    },
  }),
})

## Moving a concept up

A concept that serves more than its scope moves up, and a quick fix on its heading moves it. Which scope it moves to is the author's choice, and the protocol gives a quick fix no way to ask. So `loomMoves` offers by default the move to the nearest enclosing scope, as an action of the `refactor.move` kind that applies at once.

An editor may offer a picker of its own, a palette that lists choices and applies the one the author picks. It says so when it starts, by listing the `loom.moveConcept` command among the experimental commands of its capabilities, the convention rust-analyzer set for the commands an editor runs itself. `picksTargets` reads that list, decoded through the `ClientCommands` schema. For an editor that picks, `moveActionsOf` offers one action, which runs the command with every target, each with its title and its edit. The editor's picker then applies the target the author picks, as the Loom VS Code extension does with its quick pick.

=>

const moveCommand = 'loom.moveConcept'

const ClientCommands = Schema.Struct({
  commands: Schema.Struct({ commands: Schema.Array(Schema.String) }),
})

const picksTargets = (capabilities: ClientCapabilities | undefined): boolean =>
  Option.exists(Schema.decodeUnknownOption(ClientCommands)(capabilities?.experimental), (listed) =>
    listed.commands.commands.includes(moveCommand),
  )

const moveActionsOf =
  (frame: FrameQueryApi, picker: boolean) =>
  (move: FrameMove): ReadonlyArray<CodeAction> =>
    picker
      ? [
          {
            title: `Move \`${move.concept}\` up…`,
            kind: 'refactor.move',
            command: {
              title: `Move \`${move.concept}\` up`,
              command: moveCommand,
              arguments: [
                Array.map(move.targets, (target) => ({
                  title: target.title,
                  edit: workspaceEditOf(frame, target),
                })),
              ],
            },
          },
        ]
      : Option.toArray(
          Option.map(Array.head(move.targets), (target): CodeAction => ({
            title: target.title,
            kind: 'refactor.move',
            edit: workspaceEditOf(frame, target),
          })),
        )

const loomMoves = (frame: FrameQueryApi): LanguageServicePlugin => ({
  name: 'loom-moves',
  capabilities: { codeActionProvider: { codeActionKinds: ['refactor.move'] } },
  create: (context) => ({
    provideCodeActions: (document, range) => {
      const decoded = context.decodeEmbeddedDocumentUri(URI.parse(document.uri))
      if (!decoded || decoded[1] !== 'root') return undefined
      return Array.flatMap(
        frame.moves(frame.locationOf(decoded[0]), document.offsetAt(range.start)),
        moveActionsOf(frame, picksTargets(context.env.clientCapabilities)),
      )
    },
  }),
})

## Concepts in the symbol search

Every concept is a symbol of its workspace, so the editor's search for a symbol finds a concept by any of its forms. `loomSymbols` answers that search. It asks `FrameQuery` for the concepts whose names hold the query, and gives each as Volar's `WorkspaceSymbol`: the concept's name, the chapter that defines it as its container, and where its heading stands. Its kind is 14, the number the protocol gives a constant, since a concept means the same wherever the book uses it.

=>

const loomSymbols = (frame: FrameQueryApi): LanguageServicePlugin => ({
  name: 'loom-symbols',
  capabilities: { workspaceSymbolProvider: {} },
  create: () => ({
    provideWorkspaceSymbols: (query) =>
      Array.map(frame.symbols(query), (symbol): WorkspaceSymbol => ({
        name: symbol.name,
        kind: 14,
        containerName: symbol.container,
        location: {
          uri: frame.uriOf(symbol.location.path).toString(),
          range: symbol.location.range,
        },
      })),
  }),
})

## Titles beside numbers

An anchor may name a chapter by its number, as `::[Part II, Chapter 6, Resolving names]` does. A number names a position, so `loomHints` shows the editor what each number stands for: the chapter's title, or the part's name, as an inlay hint just after the number. It answers for the root alone, like the diagnostics, and asks `FrameQuery` for the loom's hints. Each becomes Volar's `InlayHint`, set apart from the number by a space on its left.

=>

const loomHints = (frame: FrameQueryApi): LanguageServicePlugin => ({
  name: 'loom-hints',
  capabilities: { inlayHintProvider: {} },
  create: (context) => ({
    provideInlayHints: (document) => {
      const decoded = context.decodeEmbeddedDocumentUri(URI.parse(document.uri))
      if (!decoded || decoded[1] !== 'root') return undefined
      return Array.map(frame.hints(frame.locationOf(decoded[0])), (hint): InlayHint => ({
        position: hint.position,
        label: hint.label,
        paddingLeft: true,
      }))
    },
  }),
})

## Labels as they are typed

An anchor takes a label in braces, as `::[Greeting function]{To be settled}` does. The `loomCompletions` plugin offers the labels an anchor takes while the author types between the anchor's braces. The Language Server Protocol lets a provider name the characters that trigger it, so an open brace asks for the offer at once. Each letter typed after the brace then narrows the offer. The provider answers for the root alone, like the diagnostics, and asks `FrameQuery` for the labels at the cursor. Each label becomes a `CompletionItem`, the protocol's type for one offer. The item shows the label and writes its text over the range the label replaces, so a closed pair of braces keeps its close and an open brace gains one. Its kind is 14, the number the protocol gives a keyword, since a label is a word of Loom's own.

=>

const loomCompletions = (frame: FrameQueryApi): LanguageServicePlugin => ({
  name: 'loom-completions',
  capabilities: { completionProvider: { triggerCharacters: ['{'] } },
  create: (context) => ({
    provideCompletionItems: (document, position) => {
      const decoded = context.decodeEmbeddedDocumentUri(URI.parse(document.uri))
      if (!decoded || decoded[1] !== 'root') return undefined
      return {
        isIncomplete: false,
        items: Array.map(
          frame.completions(frame.locationOf(decoded[0]), document.offsetAt(position)),
          (completion): CompletionItem => ({
            label: completion.label,
            kind: 14,
            textEdit: { range: completion.range, newText: completion.text },
          }),
        ),
      }
    },
  }),
})

## Colouring the source

A heading, an anchor, and a warp are tokens of Loom's own grammar, and no language's colouring knows them. `loomSemanticTokens` gives them their colours. Its legend names the standard token types Loom draws on — `namespace`, `variable`, `keyword`, `string`, `operator` — so the user's theme colours them with no extra setup, and the order follows `LoomSymbol`'s own, so a token's kind indexes straight into it. `provideDocumentSemanticTokens` answers for the root alone, asks `FrameQuery` for the file's coloured tokens, and turns each into Volar's tuple of line, character, length, type, and modifiers — the type an index into the legend, and no modifiers.

=>

const semanticLegend = {
  tokenTypes: [...SemanticTokenSchema.literals],
  tokenModifiers: [],
}

const loomSemanticTokens = (frame: FrameQueryApi): LanguageServicePlugin => ({
  name: 'loom-semantic-tokens',
  capabilities: { semanticTokensProvider: { legend: semanticLegend } },
  create: (context) => ({
    provideDocumentSemanticTokens: (document) => {
      const decoded = context.decodeEmbeddedDocumentUri(URI.parse(document.uri))
      if (!decoded || decoded[1] !== 'root') return undefined
      return Array.map(frame.semanticTokens(frame.locationOf(decoded[0])), (span): SemanticToken => [
        span.range.start.line,
        span.range.start.character,
        span.range.end.character - span.range.start.character,
        semanticLegend.tokenTypes.indexOf(span.type),
        0,
      ])
    },
  }),
})

## Formatting the file

`loomFormatting` answers an editor's request to format a loom. Volar formats a file's documents level by level, and the root comes first, so the Loom language formats the whole file at the root and leaves nothing for the levels below. It answers for the root alone, the document whose map to the source is the identity, so its edit lands on the file the author opened exactly as written.

It hands the root's text to `FrameQuery`'s `format`, which joins the prose and formats the code, and answers with one edit that replaces the whole text. A text that formats to itself answers with no edit, so the editor marks nothing changed.

=>

const loomFormatting = (frame: FrameQueryApi): LanguageServicePlugin => ({
  name: 'loom-formatting',
  capabilities: { documentFormattingProvider: true },
  create: (context) => ({
    provideDocumentFormattingEdits: async (document) => {
      const decoded = context.decodeEmbeddedDocumentUri(URI.parse(document.uri))
      if (!decoded || decoded[1] !== 'root') return undefined
      const text = document.getText()
      const formatted = await frame.format(frame.locationOf(decoded[0]), text)
      return formatted === text
        ? []
        : [
            {
              range: { start: document.positionAt(0), end: document.positionAt(text.length) },
              newText: formatted,
            },
          ]
    },
  }),
})

## Folding a box under its caption

A box's name is its caption, and the editor shows the caption when it folds the box away. `loomFolding` asks `FrameQuery` for the loom's boxes and gives each one a folding range, from the line that opens it to the line that closes it, with the caption as the text the editor shows in the box's place. A note without a name shows its kind. Like the diagnostics, it answers for the root alone, where the whole loom is mapped.

=>

const loomFolding = (frame: FrameQueryApi): LanguageServicePlugin => ({
  name: 'loom-folding',
  capabilities: { foldingRangeProvider: true },
  create: (context) => ({
    provideFoldingRanges: (document) => {
      const decoded = context.decodeEmbeddedDocumentUri(URI.parse(document.uri))
      if (!decoded || decoded[1] !== 'root') return undefined
      return Array.map(frame.boxes(frame.locationOf(decoded[0])), (box): FoldingRange => ({
        startLine: box.startLine,
        endLine: box.endLine,
        kind: 'region',
        collapsedText: box.caption,
      }))
    },
  }),
})

## Composing the services

`LoomLanguage` composes its plugins over the `FrameQuery` the host provides: `loomDiagnostics` for Loom's own health, `loomFixes` for the fixes a diagnostic offers, `loomDefinition` and `loomReferences` for its navigation, `loomHover` for a concept's card, `loomLinks` for the links a word two concepts claim may take, `loomApprovals` for approving a reference, `loomMoves` for moving a concept up, `loomSymbols` for the concepts a symbol search finds, `loomRename` for renaming a section, `loomSemanticTokens` for colouring its tokens, `loomHints` for the titles beside numbered references, `loomFolding` for folding each box under its caption, `loomCompletions` for the labels an anchor takes, and `loomFormatting` for formatting the file. Volar registers them all under the `loom` id.

=>

export const LoomLanguage = defineLanguageService({
  id: 'loom',
  displayName: 'Loom',
  extensions: [],
  plugins: () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const frame = yield* FrameQuery
      return [
        loomDiagnostics(frame),
        loomFixes(frame),
        loomDefinition(frame),
        loomReferences(frame),
        loomHover(frame),
        loomLinks(frame),
        loomApprovals(frame),
        loomMoves(frame),
        loomSymbols(frame),
        loomRename(frame),
        loomSemanticTokens(frame),
        loomHints(frame),
        loomFolding(frame),
        loomCompletions(frame),
        loomFormatting(frame),
      ]
    }),
})

## What this module draws on

From Volar's language service come the `LanguageServicePlugin` contract, the `ClientCapabilities` an editor states when it starts, the `CodeAction` a fix becomes, the `InlayHint` a title beside a number becomes, the `FoldingRange` a box folds into, the `CompletionItem` a label becomes, the `DocumentSymbol` a language reports for each name a document declares, the `LocationLink` a definition returns, the `Hover` a concept's card becomes, the `WorkspaceSymbol` a concept becomes in a symbol search, the `TextEdit` a rename produces, the `WorkspaceEdit` a fix that writes a new loom becomes, the `SemanticToken` tuple a colourer returns, and the editor's own `Diagnostic` type, aliased `LspDiagnostic` to keep it apart from Loom's. Effect supplies `Array`, `Option`, `pipe`, `Schema`, which decodes the commands an editor lists, and the `Effect` the service builds in. `@athrio/loom-ast` types a Loom `Diagnostic` and names the token colours in `SemanticTokenSchema`, `vscode-uri` parses an embedded document's uri, and the `LanguageService` module gives `defineLanguageService` with the `FrameQuery` capability, the `FrameLocation` navigation answers in, the `FrameEdit` a rename's span is, and the `FrameFix` and `FrameMove` a link and a move are offered as.

=>

import { Array, Effect, Option, pipe, Schema } from 'effect'
import type {
  ClientCapabilities,
  CodeAction,
  CompletionItem,
  DocumentSymbol,
  Diagnostic as LspDiagnostic,
  FoldingRange,
  Hover,
  InlayHint,
  LanguageServicePlugin,
  LocationLink,
  SemanticToken,
  TextEdit,
  WorkspaceEdit,
  WorkspaceSymbol,
} from '@volar/language-service'
import { URI } from 'vscode-uri'
import { type Diagnostic } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomNode'
import { SemanticTokenSchema } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomSymbol'
import {
  defineLanguageService,
  type FrameEdit,
  type FrameFix,
  type FrameLocation,
  type FrameMove,
  FrameQuery,
  type FrameQueryApi,
} from './LanguageService'

